      "hints": [
        "Write $\\tan^2(2x) = \\frac{\\sin^2(2x)}{\\cos^2(2x)}$ and multiply through by $\\cos(2x)$.",
        "Use $\\sin^2(2x) = 1 - \\cos^2(2x)$ to show $2\\cos^2(2x) + 3\\cos(2x) + 1 = 0$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Express $\\tan^2(2x)$ in terms of sine and cosine:\n$$\\cos(2x)\\left(\\frac{\\sin^2(2x)}{\\cos^2(2x)} + 3\\right) + 3 = 0$$",
        "Distribute $\\cos(2x)$ and simplify:\n$$\\frac{\\sin^2(2x)}{\\cos(2x)} + 3\\cos(2x) + 3 = 0$$",
        "Multiply through by $\\cos(2x)$ and substitute $\\sin^2(2x) = 1 - \\cos^2(2x)$:\n$$\\sin^2(2x) + 3\\cos^2(2x) + 3\\cos(2x) = 0$$\n$$(1 - \\cos^2(2x)) + 3\\cos^2(2x) + 3\\cos(2x) = 0$$\n$$2\\cos^2(2x) + 3\\cos(2x) + 1 = 0$$"
      ]
    },
    {
